**Answer: Yes, it is possible.**

## Proof

We construct an explicit example. Let $\widetilde{X} = \mathbb{R}^2$, which is simply connected (hence a universal covering space for any space it covers).

**Space $X = S^1 \times \mathbb{R}$.** Define the covering map
$$p_1 : \mathbb{R}^2 \to S^1 \times \mathbb{R}, \qquad p_1(t,s) = (e^{2\pi i t},\, s).$$
This is a covering map: each point $(e^{2\pi i t_0}, s_0)$ has a neighborhood $U \times V$ whose preimage is $\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} (t_0 - \epsilon, t_0+\epsilon)+n \times V$, a disjoint union of open sets each mapped homeomorphically onto $U \times V$. Since $\mathbb{R}^2$ is simply connected, it is the universal cover of $X$.

The deck transformations of $p_1$ are the maps $\varphi_n(t,s) = (t+n,\, s)$ for $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, so
$$\pi_1(X) \cong \operatorname{Deck}(p_1) \cong \mathbb{Z}.$$

**Space $Y = S^1 \times S^1 = T^2$ (the 2-torus).** Define the covering map
$$p_2 : \mathbb{R}^2 \to T^2, \qquad p_2(t,s) = (e^{2\pi i t},\, e^{2\pi i s}).$$
This is a covering map by the same argument (each point has a neighborhood whose preimage is a disjoint union of sheets). Since $\mathbb{R}^2$ is simply connected, it is the universal cover of $Y$.

The deck transformations of $p_2$ are the maps $\varphi_{m,n}(t,s) = (t+m,\, s+n)$ for $(m,n) \in \mathbb{Z}^2$, so
$$\pi_1(Y) \cong \operatorname{Deck}(p_2) \cong \mathbb{Z}^2.$$

**Comparison.** Both $X = S^1 \times \mathbb{R}$ and $Y = T^2$ have the same universal covering space $\widetilde{X} = \mathbb{R}^2$. However,
$$\pi_1(X) \cong \mathbb{Z} \quad\text{and}\quad \pi_1(Y) \cong \mathbb{Z}^2,$$
and $\mathbb{Z} \not\cong \mathbb{Z}^2$ (for instance, $\mathbb{Z}$ is cyclic while $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is not, or equivalently $\mathbb{Z}^2$ has rank 2 while $\mathbb{Z}$ has rank 1).

**Why this works conceptually.** A universal covering space $\widetilde{X}$ does not determine the base space uniquely. The base space is recovered as a quotient $\widetilde{X}/G$ where $G$ is the group of deck transformations acting freely and properly discontinuously. Different groups $G$ acting on the same $\widetilde{X}$ yield different quotients with different fundamental groups (isomorphic to the respective deck groups). Here, $\mathbb{Z}$ acts on $\mathbb{R}^2$ by translation in one coordinate (giving $S^1 \times \mathbb{R}$), while $\mathbb{Z}^2$ acts by translations in both coordinates (giving $T^2$), producing two spaces with the same universal cover but non-isomorphic fundamental groups. $\blacksquare$

